        "id": 114,
        "section": "reading",
        "topic": "Humanities",
        "passage": "PASSAGE III: Humanities\n\nA great dramatist, as Shakespeare was, could not but have felt very much hampered at being obliged continually to interrupt the progress of a play in order to send on someone to explain to the audience that the scene was to be changed to a particular place on the entrance of a particular character, and after his exit to somewhere else; that the stage was to represent the deck of a ship in a storm, or the interior of a Greek temple, or the streets of a certain town, to all of which inartistic devices Shakespeare is reduced, and for which he always amply apologizes.\n\nBesides this clumsy method, Shakespeare had two other substitutes for scenery\u2014the hanging out of a placard, and his descriptions. The first of these could hardly have satisfied his passion for picturesqueness and his feeling for beauty, and certainly did not satisfy the dramatic critic of his day. But as regards the description, to those of us who look on Shakespeare not merely as a playwright but as a poet, and who find in his plays some of the most beautiful poetry in the language, these descriptions are a source of constant delight.\n\nThey are, indeed, images which he desires the audience to see, and which he places before them with all the power of his poetic genius. He does not merely tell them that it is morning or evening; he makes them feel the morning air and see the evening shadows. In Romeo and Juliet, the dawn is not merely described; it is felt: \"Look, love, what envious streaks / Do lace the severing clouds in yonder east: / Night's candles are burnt out, and jocund day / Stands tiptoe on the misty mountain tops.\"\n\nThis is not the description of a scene-painter; it is the vision of a poet. And it is because Shakespeare's plays are filled with such visions that they have survived and will survive when all the scenery of the modern stage has been forgotten.\n\n- Shakespeare's Scenery and Poetic Vision",
        "text": "Which of the following best summarizes the main problems the author sees for Shakespeare's scenery?",
